course: dictionary
lesson: 3588
title: Fiction and non-fiction
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A fiction book is a story that a writer has made up. The people, places and events may never have happened. «The dragon flew over the castle and breathed fire» is fiction, because no dragon has ever really done that. Fiction can also mean something that is not true: «His story about a dragon was pure fiction» means he made it up.
explain: Non-fiction is the opposite. It gives true information about the real world, such as facts about animals, space or famous people. «Weddell seals keep breathing holes open in the ice» is a true fact, so you would find it in a non-fiction book.
explain: Libraries help you tell them apart. Fiction books are shelved by the author's surname. For a project, you can find facts in non-fiction books by using the library catalogue, the contents page and the index.
fact: Fiction means stories that are made up by a writer.
fact: Non-fiction gives true information about the real world.
fact: Science fiction tells stories about science and the future.
fact: Fiction books in a library are shelved by the author's surname.
fact: «Fact or fiction?» is a question to ask: is this true, or made up?
exercise: Which kind of book is a made-up story about a dragon? | Biography | *Fiction | Encyclopaedia | Non-fiction
exercise: «His story about a dragon was pure fiction.» What does fiction mean here? | A true fact from a science book | A kind of library shelf | A long, slow journey | *Something that is not true
exercise: Which book would help you find true facts about seals? | A science fiction novel about a space ship | *A non-fiction book with a contents page and an index | A picture book about a made-up dragon | A fairy tale about a magic sea king
test: In a library, how are fiction books usually sorted on the shelves? | *By the author's surname | By the colour of the cover | By the number of pages
test: What does science fiction tell stories about? | Fairy tales from long ago | Cooking and food | *Science and the future, made up | True facts about animals
exam: Is a story about a talking cat fiction or non-fiction, and how do you know? | *Fiction, because a talking cat is made up | Fiction, because it has a contents page | Non-fiction, because it is in a library | Non-fiction, because cats are real animals
